#SENSORS ARE USED TO VIEW NET TRAFFIC
    |--> IN-LINE:
          TEST ACESS POINT (TAP)
          MAN IN THE MIDDLE

        OUT OF BAND (PASSIVE)

IN-LINE SENSOR:
    | --> Placed between communicating devices to stop attacks
          Impacts network latency


PASSIVE SENSOR:
    |--> Monitors network segments
          detect attacks but cannot stop them
          copies network traffic
          Does not impact network latency


TAP: 
    |--> Best for packet collection with no data loss
          Must be placed "in line" of network traffic
          Will need several installed to capture traffic for other network segments


MITM:
  |--> Traffic capture is dependent on the attacker’s system and bandwidth
        Attackers can sniff or manipulate traffic that flows through them


SPAN:
    |--> Configured on the network Switch
          Scalable
          Places burden on the network Switch
          Can have a high degree of packet loss


FINGERPRINTING AND HOST IDENTIFICATION:
    |--> Tools used for fingerprinting and host identification can be used passively(sniffing/fingerprinting) or actively(scanning)

          FINGERPRINTING:
              ACTIVE:
              |--> Easier
                    Send packets to the target and monitor response

              PASSIVE:
              |--> More difficult
                    Rely on sniffing packets



PROTOCOL SPECIFIC IDENTIFIERS:
    |--> HTTP: USER STINGS
          SSH: INITAL CONECTION
          

POF (PASSIVE OS FINGERPRINTING)
    |--> Looks at variations in initial TTL, fragmentation flag, default IP header packet length, window size, and TCP options
          Configuration stored: /etc/p0f/p0f.fp




NETWORK BASELINING:
    |--> PREP WORK 
          Network Diagram
          Known Servers, Hosts, and Networking devices
          Known IPs, ports, and protocols
          Known forbidden IPs, ports, and protocols
          Known traffic "flows"

    |--> SCOPE AND OBJECTIVE
          What traffic/protocols to capture
          Which network segments
          Which days
          What times
          


HACKING METHODLOGIES:
    |--> FOOTPRINTING
          NETWORK SCANNING
          NET ENUMERATION
          VULNERABILITY ASSESMENT



ANOMALY DETECTION:
    |--> INDICTORS OF ATTACK:
          |--> PROACTIVE
                FOCUS ON INTENT
                CODE EXECUTION, PERSISTANCE, LAT MOVE 


|--> INDICATORS OF COMPROMISE:
      |--> REACTIVE 
            CYBER EVIDENCE
            PROVIDE INFORMATION THAT CAN CHANGE 
                MALWARE,IP ADDRESSES, EXPLOITS, SIGNATURES


EXTRA INDICATORS:
    |--> .EXE/ EXE FILES
          NOP SLED
          WELL KNOW SIGNATURES
          MISMATCHED PROTOCOL
          UNUSUAL TRAFFIC 
          HEAVY TRAFFIC FLOW AT UNUSUAL TIMES


SIGNS OF INDICATORS OF ATTACK:
    |--> REMOTE LOGINS 
          HIGH AMOUNTS OF THE SAME PROTOCOLS
          MALWARE REINFECTION
          NET SCANS



SIGNS OF INDICATORS OF COMPROMISE:
    |--> HEAVY OUTBOUND TRAFFIC 
          LARGE RESPONSES OF HTML 
          HIGH NUMBER OF REQUEST OF SIMIALAR FILES 
          REGISTRY CHANGES
          UNKNOWN PATCHING LOCATIONS



ICMP TUNNELING:
    |--> ICMP TYPE 8 AND TYPE 0
          BOTH SHOULD BE 1 FOR 1
          SAME SIZE AND PAYLOAD 
                LOOK OUT FOR:
                  |--> REQUEST/REPLY IMBALENCE
                        ABNORMAL/ DIFFERENT PAYLOADS 
                        #LOOK INTO HEX FOR MESSEAGES OUTSIDE THE SCOPE OF IMCP


DNS TUNNELING:
    |--> 1 TO 1 QUERY AND RESPONSE
          THINGS TO LOOK FOR:
            |--> QUERY/ RESPONSE IMBALENCE 
                  ABNORMAL/DIFFERENT PAYLOAD
                  CONTINUOUS QUERIES


HTTP(S) TUNNELING:
    |--> CLIENT REQUEST/ SERVER RESPONDS
          THINGS TO LOOK OUT FOR:
              |--> STEADY CONNECTIONS OVER LONG PERIODS
                    CHECK SESSION ESTABLIHMENT FOR ABNORMALITIES



BEACONING: 
    |--> CALL BACK TO COMMAND AND CONTROL LOCATION
          GETS/ SENDS COMMANDS FROM/ TO COMMAND AND CONTROL
            THINGS TO LOOK FOR:
                |--> BEACON TIMING:
                      COMMONLY AT REGULAR INTERVALS
        


    

            

          
      










    
